# Modules, interfaces, and data

This English guidance derives from sections 3–7 of the technical design
baseline v0.2. The [preserved Chinese export](模块接口与数据.md) and its
[source provenance](模块接口与数据.provenance.json) identify the original version
and selected sections. Use the technical template selected for the run to
write the requested document language.

## 3. Architecture context and module boundaries

Identify system boundaries and where the client, local processes, files and
external services run. Record actors, external systems, input sources and
untrusted inputs. Show main data flows and call directions, including
persistence, model or API calls, export and user-visible results. Explain the
module dependencies and why they are needed, alternatives and decisions,
single points of failure, isolation, failure propagation, and traceability to
the product commitments in section 2. Link each diagram boundary to a module
card or interface.

Divide by stable responsibility, data ownership, trust boundary and reason
for change. Assign each necessary action one primary owner. Distinguish
business rules, interaction and display, persistence, external services and
knowledge governance. These boundaries can share a process. Tailor granularity
to the risks and the ability to verify and recover. Explain broad
abstractions, redundant wrappers or modules without an immediate use.

Name the owners of data writes, state transitions and error handling.
Require callers to use the owner's contract and observable success result.
Keep dependency direction clear and avoid cycles. Where callbacks or
events are needed, define ownership, ordering, duplicates, failure and
consistency. Access shared support through interfaces. Knowledge governance
owns sources, versions, adoption and scope. Product rules and stage decisions
have their own authorities.

Check that the complete set of modules covers requirements and data flows.
Complete the section 4 cards and resolve gaps or conflicting ownership.
The Chinese export cites historical S01, S05 and S06 through its reading
index and cites RS04 and RS05 for engineering and design review. The source
provenance identifies the unbundled references. Treat these citations as
method background. Choose architecture for the current project and its
constraints.

## 4. Module cards and collaboration review

Complete a card for every module in scope. Refer to shared details through
stable IDs and size the card by responsibility and risk. Describe enough
behavior to determine inputs, state changes, reads and writes, call order,
failure feedback and assertions.

Record the following in each card.

- Source requirement, feature or product behavior, acceptance and delivery
  purpose with IDs and versions. State responsibility, exclusions, guarantees,
  provider and callers, process or environment and position in the actual user
  path.
- Input types, required and nullable fields, range, size, format, version,
  source and validation failure. Output type, success semantics, partial
  result indicators, user feedback and return timing.
- Provided and called interface IDs, contract versions, synchronous or
  asynchronous calls and call order. Cover initial, running, waiting,
  completed, failed, canceled and recovery states with entry and exit
  conditions or a reason for inapplicability.
- Owned and read data IDs, location, sole writer, consistency, retention and
  deletion. State actor, project and material scope, authorization checks,
  credential handles, outbound data and side effects.
- Normal processing, rules, commit boundaries and scenario IDs. Record errors,
  visible feedback, responsible owner, retained originals or intermediate
  results and downstream effects. Define cancellation points, results after
  commit, duplicate detection, idempotency scope and uncertain outcomes.
- Checkpoints, restart prerequisites, reauthorization and version checks,
  startup and exit order and the failure exit.
- Dependencies and versions, caller and provider prerequisites,
  configuration, source of defaults and when they apply. For needed time,
  storage, memory or concurrency constraints, state units, scale, threshold
  source and measurement method. Keep unsupported limits pending.
- Primary feasibility evidence with source, version, locator, conclusion
  and limits. Identify hypothesis, trial and actual evidence. Retain
  alternatives, selection rationale, costs, risks, revisit conditions and
  effective user decisions.
- Task IDs, entry conditions, dependencies, outputs and allowed scope.
  Link normal, counterexample and failure assertions to acceptance and test
  IDs, applicable platforms and evidence methods. Record open items, affected
  tasks, next action, owner, closure and resume conditions.

For each provider and caller, compare the interface, data or event ID,
provider guarantee and caller expectation. Resolve any conflict. Check that
input limits have handling, success is observable, ownership matches the
writer, failure has an owner and an exit, and tests cover actual behavior.
A complete card can document a blocker. Execution waits for critical unknowns
to be resolved.

## 5. Interfaces, calls, and errors

Record each interface ID and version, provider, caller and process, file or
external service boundary. Describe input fields, types, ranges, nullability,
sizes, version, encoding and source validation. Describe output types and
semantics for success, partial completion, waiting and durable commit. Pin
preconditions, postconditions, required state and permissions, observable
results and call order. Include synchronous, asynchronous, event or streaming
behavior as applicable, including concurrency and races.

Define error codes and when the caller should retry, clarify, reauthorize,
recover or stop. Record timeout basis, attempt and cost limits, cancellation,
duplicate recognition and handling of uncertain outcomes. Identify the source
of actor identity, scope, outbound data and the actual read or execution
authorization check. Cover compatibility for field changes, old callers and
old data, with clear feedback for incompatibility. Verify the contract on both
sides against module cards, scenarios and assertions.

Use pseudotypes, tables or state and sequence diagrams to describe input,
output and errors. Distinguish receipt of an async request from saved success.
Resolve the outcome or qualify a safe retry before repeating a potentially
chargeable or side-effecting request.

For each error ID, specify its trigger, owner, visible result, data and
side-effect status, conditions for retry, cancel or recover, downstream
effects and tests. Protect credentials and unauthorized data in error output.
Define the outcome of cancellation racing with commit. Analyze effects on
consumers, data, tests and user decisions when the contract changes.

## 6. Runtime scenarios, states, and sequence

Select representative complete user paths. Cover applicable normal operation,
startup and exit, cancellation, partial failure, duplicates and recovery.
Expand background concurrency, long-running work, offline or disconnected
behavior, external APIs and multiple windows where capabilities and risks
warrant it.

For each scenario ID, record the product path, requirement and acceptance,
environment, prior state, inputs and authorization. Trace user actions and
module calls in order. Show intermediate data and state, temporary versus
committed data, and waiting versus partial completion. Describe success and
visible feedback, errors, cancellation, timeout, duplicates, exit, restart
and when recovery or a new selection is needed. Record the evidence plan and
actual status.

For each transition, record the object, current state, trigger and
conditions, checks and calls, next state, data and side effects, visible
result and exit when the transition fails. Give key reachable states a clear
exit. Check commit semantics in success and failure. After cancellation,
stop further calls outside the effective authorization. Ensure duplicate
operations avoid duplicate records and charges and that visible state
matches actual state. Verify reachability and reliability as required. A
state table records the design.

## 7. Data model, storage, and lifecycle

For each data ID, record fields, types, ranges and sourced defaults, primary
keys, relationships, uniqueness and invariants, including cross-object
constraints and version relationships. Identify ownership, readers, sole
writer and project isolation. Distinguish temporary, durable, backup and
cache storage, with location, format and version. Specify consistency,
commit, concurrency and data retained after failure.

Cover creation, update, retention, deletion, recovery and cleanup. Trace
derived index, cache, summary, export and citation invalidation. Define the
scope of compatibility, migration and rollback. For corruption, storage
exhaustion or denied access, record user feedback, preservation of originals
and recovery steps. Record actual evidence, assumptions and verification for
storage guarantees using the applicable source or trial.

State when the application can report a save and what survives interruption.
Specify how to restore a backup and separately verify restoration. For
deletion, distinguish stopping future use, deleting controlled objects,
cleaning derived data, historical references and the limits on remote copies
already sent. Verify that data relationships, normal and failure reads and
writes, retention, recovery and migration agree with the scenario and
delivery scope.
